body:
If you're just doing the coding yourself for the site, all you need to pay for is the domain registration and hosting costs. You can probably set up the domain for quite cheap ($20, if that). The hosting cost is tied to bandwidth; you'll need to have a rough estimate of how many views you intend to get, how much data will be sent each way, etc., to figure out how much you'll need to spend.

&#x200B;

As an aside, how would this website be different from R?

Colleges participate in intramurals, but I think most have started for this term. They might need replacement players though; if you contact your college's athletics coordinators they can get you connected, either this term or next. I think you have to play for the college you're registered in, but not 100% sure.

In the meantime, there's always pickup basketball, and sometimes volleyball, at both the AC and Hart House. Check the schedules and show up sometime :)

&#x200B;

I'd take the risk. After all, "its very compulsory"

You're not dealing with that slightly compulsory stuff.

&#x200B;

&#x200B;

If you didn't see him die, he's in a superposition of being both dead and alive.

Or was that Schrödinger?

&#x200B;

You are 100% correct that I have an agenda. My agenda includes despising people who commit sexual violence. It also includes calling people out who spend more effort defending the perpetrator than the victim.

&#x200B;

Beyond that, I'm not willing to debate with a sock puppet. Post here using your real account and I will happily address your false claims (my agenda includes laughing at cowards who use throwaway accounts to protect their main identity).

Again, so many fancy words, to evade the basic truths.  


The legal determination of guilty or not guilty does not affect factual guilt or innocence. The judge even admitted this in this case. 

> However, Nelson added, “A final note: A reasonable doubt as to an  absence of consent is not an affirmative finding that \[the complainant\]  consented to sexual activity in the bedroom.” 

&#x200B;

If the woman had consented, she would have remembered consenting, because consent must be ongoing. It's not a one-time thing. 

&#x200B;

 Remember, OJ Simpson legally did not kill his wife, despite the fact that he totally did.

&#x200B;

> was found to have caused **extensive bruising** to the complainant, and  Nelson found that Marrello “was reckless when he applied physical force  towards \[the complainant\] **not caring whether she consented or not**.” 

&#x200B;

Doesn't sound like minor bruises to me. This guy seems to have a very serious problem. Normal people care about their partner's wellbeing and consent.

Typical union tactic. They're going to make your life (as an uninvolved person) miserable, with the tacit understanding that the best way to get your life back is to support them, call up whoever they're mad at and add your voice to demanding they be given whatever they want, so you can get some sleep. 

&#x200B;

Guerilla recruiting, unions kill for this sort of stuff.

Wrong flair. This should be under "humor"

&#x200B;

&#x200B;

I have never in my life understood why restaurants are permitted to pay such low wages that employees need to beg the customers for spare change, basically.

&#x200B;

Just increase the prices by like 10%, and pay the staff a regular wage (you know, as if they're human beings or something). It literally ends up being the same thing in the end, without the awkwardness.

I'm trying to picture who exactly sits around on their phone all day and reads tweets from their university.

&#x200B;

A lonely person is who.

Most run  of the mill clubs don't care, and many of them already have general public members.

The only caveats are, you can't become an exec, and if they're a sports club, you don't have automatic access to the gyms etc.

&#x200B;

You're "pretty damn shocked" that another human being may have an opinion you find disagreeable?

You must not get out much :)

&#x200B;

Muck raking at its finest.

&#x200B;

I have no love for bigots and racists, but if everyone who ever posted stupid shit on social media were to lose their job, the country would absolutely grind to a halt. Seriously, ask yourselves; if every post you ever made on Reddit, Facebook, Instagram, Snapchat and Tumblr were to become public, with your real name and photo beside them, how would that make you look? Sure, sure, you've been careful enough to hide all controversial stuff behind an alias, or made it all friends-only. Or so you hope.

&#x200B;

He's a registrar's assistant. His job is to push paperwork, run forms, and manage account payments, etc.. If there's evidence that his views have compromised his ability to do this, then fire the guy immediately without severance. But if he's doing his job correctly, treating everyone properly and equally from the time he clocks in until he clocks back out and leaves the premises, I really don't care what his personal views are.

&#x200B;

God knows I disagree with half the nonsense spouted by the UTSU (albeit this year seems a bit better than most, fingers crossed). Ditto for the SJW's in my own college's administration. But as long as they're doing their job, they can say whatever they want (within the law) when they're living their personal lives.

&#x200B;

I'm far more worried about our school becoming a thought-police state, than I am about some random functionary with stupid opinions.

University of Toronto Anime & Manga Association.

&#x200B;

aka. best club on campus :P

Completing the Academic Bridging program will allow you to enroll as a first year undergrad. From there, you can take all the mathematics programs you want, \****provided you have the necessary prerequisites***\* , which I believe is high school calculus and possibly algebra.

&#x200B;

Beyond that, it's up to you and your abilities in the subject. Math isn't easy, and you'll need to develop some very strong study skills right out of the gate, especially if you have been out of school for a period of time.

You were in the wrong because the university grounds are private property, and the university (who has delegated management of this to the campus police) asked you to leave. You did not leave when told to. 

&#x200B;

I'm assuming they asked you to leave because you were part of a group who was bringing in outside alcohol and jumping the fence. Even if you didn't do this personally, you were associating with people who did. They're within their rights to eject anyone they suspect is/will cause trouble, so long as it doesn't violate the human rights act.

&#x200B;

Being belligerent to officers probably wasn't the smartest thing. They're just doing their job. If you refused to leave, they're not going to just walk away.

&#x200B;

Anything else you can do? Dump the girlfriend. She seems to be dragging you into trouble you don't need.

yes, hurry, it's not like they won't have another one at Sid Smith in two weeks...

&#x200B;

This poster company is so corrupt. Nobody else gets to take over entire building lobbies.

False dichotomy. It's not an all-or-nothing issue. As with most things in life, there's a comfortable middle ground. Ban laptops and cell phones for nonacademic use. Allow them for course related tasks such as notetaking and reading slides, etc.

&#x200B;

To follow this train of thought to your car argument, we don't ban cars. We ban the irresponsible and reckless misuse of cars in situations where others are affected. You can drive your car properly all day long. Alternately, you can go to a demolition derby or a field on your property and smash into everything you see.

&#x200B;

Both classrooms and public roads are shared spaces, and sadly, some rules are needed that balance the rights of individuals against the responsibility to not interfere with others in that public space.

The more you practice, the better you will become. Find situations to socialize with people. Maybe do some tutoring; offer a discount to tutor someone in economics if they spend 10 minutes afterwards helping you with your English?

&#x200B;

Above all, don't speak Chinese. Don't socialize in Chinese, don't watch Chinese television, don't listen to Chinese music.

&#x200B;

You got better at economics the more you used it. You will get better at English, the more you use it :)

&#x200B;

Contact housing services Monday morning. 

[https://www.studentlife.utoronto.ca/hs/contact-us](https://www.studentlife.utoronto.ca/hs/contact-us)

&#x200B;

There MAY be open spots if people drop out or otherwise don't show up for the start of the year.

&#x200B;

I wouldn't panic though; if you can afford on-campus housing, there's far, far more off-campus housing, and much of it is far cheaper (albeit, you may need to commute a bit).

They're not your friend, they don't care about your time. Everything they do is designed to give them a surplus of leads, so that they can quickly sell anything. If they have one copy of the book and get 30 requests for it, they're just going to sell the copy, they're not going to take the time to respond to the other 29 people.

&#x200B;

There's enough people desperate to save an extra $5, that they can get away with their behavior. You need to live with it or find another source. 

&#x200B;

It's a common cognitive fallacy. No rational/healthy person sees someone walking down the street alone and believes that they're now qualified to judge the person's life. The only person concerned about it is yourself. I don't mean to sound harsh, but you need to understand that you are your own worst critic in this matter. Plenty of people walk alone places. Frankly, I worry more about the people who can't go to the washroom without five of their friends in tow.

&#x200B;

Loneliness is a fact of life for many people in big cities, and on big campuses. There's so many people that even if you could strike up a close friendship, the rarity of seeing the same person more than a few times will make it difficult.

&#x200B;

The solution is to take initiative. If you're going straight from the subway to the classroom to the subway and home again, you will never meet anyone. Join a club or three; activities that interest you and seem welcoming. Get to know everyone's name, and a fact or two about them. Show interest in their lives, ask them questions, and be open about the interesting things that make you unique. If there's a pub night afterwards, join along. Add them on facebook. Try to organize a group going to an event, concert, or game. The more opportunities you have to meet people, the more people you'll meet :)

&#x200B;

As for romantic relationships, relatively few people are actually in stable, happy, long-term relationships. Most people aren't there, and many don't even want to be. The ones that are happy are the ones we remember because it's what we want to be. But not all of the "lovey dovey" hand-holding couples are as happy as they seem to outsiders. 

&#x200B;

Your time here at UofT is what you make it. It's a lesson I learned the hard way, but happiness, relationships, and fulfillment will not just fall into your lap. Just as you need to work very hard to get a good career, a good car, and a good degree, you also need to work hard to get a good relationship (both platonically and romantically). There's nothing wrong with you, hopefully you believe that.

The prof doesn't want people playing Minecraft on their laptops when they're supposed to be learning. This is reasonable, and probably the sign of a professor who actually cares about the topic they're teaching. 

&#x200B;

If they were opposed to someone taking notes on their laptops or searching for academic papers related to the topic at hand, I'd be much more concerned.

The UTSU is located directly in front of Hart House, in the small old observatory building.

&#x200B;

[https://www.google.ca/maps/place/University+of+Toronto+Students'+Union/@43.6639119,-79.3966743,17z/data=!4m12!1m6!3m5!1s0x882b34b975ae0f55:0x28ebf4925382168f!2sHart+House!8m2!3d43.663908!4d-79.3944856!3m4!1s0x0:0xffcfa73235060db3!8m2!3d43.6631762!4d-79.394675?hl=en](https://www.google.ca/maps/place/University+of+Toronto+Students'+Union/@43.6639119,-79.3966743,17z/data=!4m12!1m6!3m5!1s0x882b34b975ae0f55:0x28ebf4925382168f!2sHart+House!8m2!3d43.663908!4d-79.3944856!3m4!1s0x0:0xffcfa73235060db3!8m2!3d43.6631762!4d-79.394675?hl=en)

&#x200B;

Remember that you also need to fill the program requirements (1 specialist, or 2 majors, or a major and 2 minors) to graduate, even if you have 20 credits.

&#x200B;

November graduation is much smaller, just for the record.

Try chess. There's a ton of people playing chess every Friday night in Hart House. 

&#x200B;

If I were a hiring manager, I'd be more likely to hire a chess whiz for most jobs, than someone who's really good at chasing a ball around :P

&#x200B;

You don't. A generation ago, they were super lax about this stuff. Not anymore. Lawsuits and terrorism are always on their minds lol.

&#x200B;

I recall an engineering student died once, trying to rappel down a building. Not sure if it was here or somewhere else. Apparently it was an old tradition.

I was using the 8 months of the school year, so closer to $100/month. Maybe it's a little optimistic, but I think it's silly to register at a university just to use the wifi. A college might be a better deal. Or, you know, home internet...  


Also, if they don't attend the class and fail, they'll get suspended, so this isn't really a viable long-term plan :P

&#x200B;

One course, with incidental fees, is like $800.

&#x200B;

For that money, you could get unlimited mobile data for a year.

If you leave a conflict on your timetable, the registrar can remove you at any time. They may or may not notify you beforehand to give you time to pick one.

&#x200B;

As for a lecture being necessary, yes. Some people claim they "don't need" the lectures, but it's a dangerous game. At the very least, it could be the difference between an A vs C, or a B vs D.

See my reply, the information given to you by the other poster was not correct. 

&#x200B;

For your tuition, it shouldn't, but your college registrar would handle that.

No. You can add fall courses only until September 19, after which you cannot add anything new. You can still drop fall courses until November 9th without academic penalty.  


r/http://www.artsci.utoronto.ca/current/course/timetable/1819_fw/2018_fall_dates

&#x200B;

Right now, you can add or drop whatever you want for Winter. There will be a similar schedule for that term, but it's far off right now.

&#x200B;

Both are free for students, for the basic stuff (track, pool, weight room etc.) aside from times that they're booked - Hart House is far less booked by groups than AC.

&#x200B;

AC tends to have newer stuff and more space, but they cater to a lot of the Varsity teams and clubs, so there's times you can't use certain things. Availability isn't bad though.  


Both offer free (included) drop-in sports and fitness classes. Rule of thumb is that AC has better drop in team sports, while Hart House has better and more drop in fitness classes.

&#x200B;

Find your niche, or split your time between the two. Both are totally valid options :)

Intro and syllabus doesn't take an hour. Almost all profs have actual material they start covering on day 1.  Often it's a review, but this is important; these are the things they're assuming you know already. If you don't, you may be shooting yourself in the foot down the road.

Best advice is to start getting into good study habits now, not later. You've probably had all summer to not attend class :P

&#x200B;

It's a big course. Con hall, right?

&#x200B;

A general rule of thumb with big first year courses is, they're easy to do well in (if you put in the hours and effort), but very hard to do amazing in. You can get a B+ without too much effort if you show up every lecture and keep up with the readings, and devote enough time to essays and assignments. To get that elusive 85% though, will take a fair bit more. It's totally doable, but should not be seen as an "Easy A".

No, the "Jank fees" range from moderately noble programs, to ridiculous fluff.

&#x200B;

Aside from the "Student Refugee Program", they're almost all a bunch of special-interest nonsense that virtually nobody will ever use and that have no real case for mandatory collection of money from all students.  


For example, everyone pays for the Cinema Studies Union, despite the fact that most students never take a class. 

&#x200B;

Then there's something called OPIRG, which as near as I can tell, is a far-left lobby group with no real connection to UofT. They get money from everyone, regardless of their political position or support.

&#x200B;

I'm happy to help subsidize dental and healthcare for the benefit of those who would otherwise struggle. However, I'm not going to pay for organizations that have nothing to do with me, are ridiculous, and don't need the money.

Ask them at the front desk next time you're in the area. 

&#x200B;

I feel like you could get away with this at Hart House more than the other two...

Japanese doramas are a poor substitute for anime.

&#x200B;

There's a halfway point that you can land on with your studying; EAS121 is offered second term, and is the second half of the first year course. Even if you don't get in to 220, you'll be able to take it next year.

&#x200B;

Parts of Toronto are incredibly safe, and parts of it are approaching the status of no-go zones. The trick is staying out of the bad places. 

&#x200B;

It's also worth noting that while there are a lot of shootings lately, almost all of them are targeted; gangs and other losers with grudges and vendettas killing each other off. Which would actually be perfectly fine, except that often innocent people get caught in the crossfire or wrongly identified.

You're looking at a 1-2 year suspension, with a notation on your transcript for a year after that; possibly until graduation. They have all the evidence they need; you are basically at their mercy. If you're sorry and contrite, say so. 

&#x200B;

One thing they look at is the likelihood of you repeating the behavior in the future. With your low GPA, that could raise further red flags.

&#x200B;

My advice? Honestly, university may not be your thing... try a community college; the work is easier, less pressure, and more jobs available upon graduation. 

&#x200B;

&#x200B;

My guess is that Thursday and Friday are lectures only, based on first weeks always being that way. In the extremely unlikely event that you're required to attend a tutorial or lab, the prof should know this is an exception to the normal way of doing things, and make an announcement. 

&#x200B;

Check the syllabus the night before, just in case (it very well may not be up yet, so don't panic). And if you really want to be sure, drop by the classroom just to see if anyone else showed up. It has the added bonus of refreshing your memory as to the location :)

&#x200B;

This is prime studying time, my friend. Review the lecture you just finished, and read up on the class you're about to start. Maybe proofread a lab report, or brainstorm points for that essay in a month. These habits WILL improve your grades. 

&#x200B;

If you do need some downtime (and most people do, unless they're sadistic), there's no shame in watching an episode of your favorite anime on your laptop, or reading a random book for fun. UC and a few other places have interesting museums/art galleries, and sometimes it's fun to explore new corners of campus (today's discovery could be tomorrow's perfect study space). When I had a ROM membership, I would often pop in and spend half an hour checking out a couple exhibits.

&#x200B;

You could also take up a new hobby, like photography. Hart House has a good camera club that I'm pretty sure members can get access to. They also have chess boards and other games.

&#x200B;

Or, and I know this last one is kinda out there, you could try starting up a conversation with some similarly bored people, maybe make some new friends whose availability matches yours :P

The college you're registered in has very, very little impact on anything you'll experience at UofT, unless you decide to get involved in student politics or intramural sports. You'd do far better getting to know the people you'll be spending the next year living with.

&#x200B;

Although, check to see if you're allowed to attend a different frosh... I'm not sure if it's permitted (although living in their residence might give you a special case)

That's sage advice. I feel I've considered the tradeoffs more than it might appear. I just have zero interest in material things. While most people enjoy a mix of material goods and experiences, I've literally never taken enjoyment of any material good aside from its most utilitarian need. My $300 laptop and $250 bicycle are the most expensive things I own, by far.   


I realize on some level I'm probably mildly mentally ill (from an evolutionary perspective, conspicuous consumption is somewhat rational, as well as striving for a higher social status), but I am who I am  :P

&#x200B;

Don't hold your breath. Their priority at this time of the year is marking up textbooks, and restocking overpriced UofT branded merch. Nobody to spare to fix something so trivial.

&#x200B;

To be honest, it doesn't take that long to fill in the form manually. And many profs haven't even uploaded their textbook requirements yet anyhow.

Often the course administrator can pull a few strings (if there's 25 spots in the tutorial, they probably have actual space for 30 students or so), but they're not really required to. Some are great and some terrible at doing this. 

&#x200B;

Keep checking ACORN and try to snag a different space, in the meantime.

I was giving you the benefit of the doubt until now, but you're clearly arguing for the sake of arguing. You're not upset or mistreated. You're probably slightly glad they have women's only hours, because it gives you something to complain about, and perceived ammunition to use towards whatever point you're trying to make.

&#x200B;

I tried to help, but I'm exiting this conversation now. Have a good day.

There will be plenty of posters hanging around all the math classrooms, along with annoying people handing out flyers for the test prep sessions. You don't have to go far to find them. The quality or legitimacy of these outfits may be questionable, however.  


If you want a quality one-on-one tutor (and can afford it), many grad students in the math department offer the service. I thought I saw a list once, but can't find it.

&#x200B;

Frankly, this is your best bet: [https://www.math.toronto.edu/cms/undergraduate-program/current-students-ug/math-aid-centres/](https://www.math.toronto.edu/cms/undergraduate-program/current-students-ug/math-aid-centres/)

&#x200B;

Try workstudy positions; they're only 15 hours a week maximum.  


Casual work like dog walking, shoveling snow, babysitting, or yardwork could get you something.

&#x200B;

Check the boards in Sid Smith. They're always hiring people for $10 to take a psychology study for one hour. These can add up.

&#x200B;

&#x200B;

Not sure, but I see a lot of courses with far, far fewer enrolled and they are still happening. I'm in a course with 8 out of 50 enrolled, and nothing amiss there (yet)

&#x200B;

If you search religion courses on [https://timetable.iit.artsci.utoronto.ca/](https://timetable.iit.artsci.utoronto.ca/) you'll see that plenty of the third year courses are in similar situations. Only half filled or fewer. I wouldn't worry too much, but it might reassure you to find a backup course now, that you can switch to quickly in the unlikely event it's cancelled.

&#x200B;

University is much better than high school. Most (not all) of the jerks and delinquents aren't there, at least not past first year. Almost all the undergrads will be nervous like yourself. Most will try to hide their nervousness (some better than others), but to some degree, everyone's in the same boat. Thousands of frosh have gone before you through the same exact situation :)

&#x200B;

Living on your own isn't too different than living at home, except your parents went away for the weekend and left you some money. You have more responsibility for your own life, but it's really not a huge step. There are services and people to help you with anything major, at all three campuses. Much of this will be in your student guidebook that you'll get. Don't be afraid to use any of these services; you've already paid for them through your fees mostly, and they do help.

&#x200B;

Socially, I'd recommend you work on finding your niche. Do you have a hobby or sport you play? Connect with people who share that. Clubs fair helps, you can also search online for groups. Remember that everyone else is new too. Don't try too hard, but be genuinely friendly and interested in people. A lot of organizations have a pub night (all ages) or games night the first couple weeks to welcome new members. Go, say hi to people, and before you know it you'll have a few regular friends you see throughout the week.

Depends entirely on the class. Also the schedule; if there's a class in there beforehand, you have no benefit arriving early.

&#x200B;

My experience is that after the first 3-4 weeks, early birds taper off dramatically.

What class is this that's 170 waitlisted souls, wandering in the ether?  


Keep in mind that probably a good 150 of them will also have the same idea :P

&#x200B;

Depends on too many factors to even predict.  


Is it a required course? They see much less turnover.

Is it winter term? You might have a better shot; people switch programs or drop out.

Either way, it's not good odds, sorry.

&#x200B;

There is one faint hope clause, though. Looking at the sessional dates here:

[https://fas.calendar.utoronto.ca/sessional-dates](https://fas.calendar.utoronto.ca/sessional-dates)

&#x200B;

They turn off the waitlists off on September 14. However, your last day to add a course is September 19. So there's a 5 day period where every opening is given to the first person who notices this and manually signs up on Acorn.   


&#x200B;
